WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.430
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about Amazon CloudFront.</v>

2
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.243
CloudFront is a content delivery network, CDN,

3
00:00:05.243 --> 00:00:07.172
and it improves the read performance

4
00:00:07.172 --> 00:00:08.839
of whatever you have in the backend

5
00:00:08.839 --> 00:00:12.510
because the content is going to be cached at the edge.

6
00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.904
You have over 200 points of presence globally

7
00:00:14.904 --> 00:00:17.310
and it protects you against DDoS attacks

8
00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.470
because it is distributed at the edge.

9
00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:23.423
It has integration with Shield, WAF, and Route 53.

10
00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:25.620
Any application on CloudFront

11
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.290
is going to be exposed through HTTPS,

12
00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.450
and then internally you can talk to HTTP

13
00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.590
or HTTPS to access any kind of internal applications.

14
00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.020
There's also support for the WebSocket protocol.

15
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:37.853
So how does that look?

16
00:00:37.853 --> 00:00:39.750
Well, we have an application, say for example,

17
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.775
we deploy a website or files onto an S3 bucket.

18
00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:45.330
We want global user to access them.

19
00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:49.800
Then a user in America can access a cache in America,

20
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.740
which is going to make a request to the S3 buckets.

21
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.390
And then after it is going to cache

22
00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.490
the result of that request in America

23
00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:57.840
so that subsequent requests

24
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.060
do not have to go back to the S3 bucket.

25
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.970
And similarly, for a user in China,

26
00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.980
we'll go through a point of presence in Asia

27
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.720
to access your S3 bucket.

28
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.360
So what are the origins of CloudFront?

29
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.590
Well, we have an S3 bucket,

30
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.070
and this is used for distributing files from S3 to the web

31
00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.830
or back from the web to S3

32
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.800
to upload them using CloudFront as an ingress.

33
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.650
And the security is maintained and guaranteed

34
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.761
through the use of the CloudFront Origin Access Control

35
00:01:25.761 --> 00:01:28.050
or OAC feature.

36
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.600
You can also use CloudFront as a MediaStore container

37
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.550
and MediaPackage endpoint.

38
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.980
This is used to deliver video on demand, VOD,

39
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.420
or live streaming videos using AWS Media Services.

40
00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.890
You also have VPC Origin

41
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.228
for application hosted in VPC private subnets,

42
00:01:43.228 --> 00:01:45.686
and this is to distribute content hosted

43
00:01:45.686 --> 00:01:48.240
through an application load balancer,

44
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.480
a network load balancer, or directly on EC2 instances,

45
00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.000
but all of that in private networks.

46
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.480
You can also use a custom origin, which is HTTP based.

47
00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.280
So this is for anything that's public,

48
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.560
so you could use it on top of an API gateway

49
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.610
if you wanted more control.

50
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.460
Otherwise, you can use straight from API gateway,

51
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.121
the API gateway edge feature,

52
00:02:07.121 --> 00:02:10.290
or you can use it for example, on an S3 bucket

53
00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:11.760
that's configured as a website.

54
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.300
First, you need to EM enable a static website hosting

55
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.952
or finally any HTP backend you want that is public hosted in

56
00:02:18.952 --> 00:02:21.573
or outside of AWS.

57
00:02:22.679 --> 00:02:25.800
So what if you use S3 as an origin?

58
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.900
Well, we have an Origin S3 bucket,

59
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.840
and again, I told you there is tight integration

60
00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.260
between CloudFront and USU bucket by using OAC.

61
00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.570
So the origin access control.

62
00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.910
So this ensures that then the content

63
00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:39.990
of your SU bucket is going

64
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.690
to be cached at the edge in various locations.

65
00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:46.778
For example, Los Angeles, for example, Sao Paolo, Mumbai,

66
00:02:46.778 --> 00:02:49.470
and then Melbourne and the public.

67
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.420
The idea is that all the requests made

68
00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.080
to these edge locations if they have cash content will be

69
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.110
served from the edge, not from the S3 bucket in the origin.

70
00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.360
Therefore it improves to you the latency

71
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.900
and also the number of requests made on your S3 buckets.

72
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.171
So what is the difference between CloudFront

73
00:03:05.171 --> 00:03:07.560
and S3 cross reunification?

74
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.990
Well, with CloudFront you get a Global Edge network

75
00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:13.590
and files are cached for a time to live maybe a day,

76
00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:14.879
and it's great for static content

77
00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:17.580
that must be available everywhere.

78
00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.030
If you use S3 cross region replication, you must set up it

79
00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:24.565
for every ser, every region you want to replicate to,

80
00:03:24.565 --> 00:03:27.990
and the files are going to be updated in near real time.

81
00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:28.890
It's read only.

82
00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.710
And so it's great if you have dynamic content that needs

83
00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:34.470
to be available at low latency in the few regions.

84
00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.840
So they have a very different use cases.

85
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.596
So if you want to use the VPC origin functionality,

86
00:03:39.596 --> 00:03:42.120
you can actually get content directly

87
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.430
from your private subnets into CloudFront

88
00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.980
using what's called a private origin.

89
00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:48.810
So that means that you can deliver traffic

90
00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.690
to private applicational balancers, network balancers,

91
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.345
and EC2 instances.

92
00:03:53.345 --> 00:03:55.172
So CloudFront will connect

93
00:03:55.172 --> 00:03:58.483
through something called the VPC origin that we must set up,

94
00:03:58.483 --> 00:04:00.538
and then we have optimal security

95
00:04:00.538 --> 00:04:03.107
and an optimal setup for this.

96
00:04:03.107 --> 00:04:05.670
So this is to me the best way of doing things,

97
00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.690
but you may want to know how to do it also using public IPS

98
00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.150
and public ARBs and eStore as origins.

99
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.100
So this is what I'm gonna show you is just the old way

100
00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.240
of doing things,

101
00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.220
but good to know from an exam perspective

102
00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.239
because the exam might be tricky.

103
00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:23.700
You can also use CloudFront with an origin of HTP types,

104
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.150
for example, an EC2 instance or an applicational balancer.

105
00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.780
So in this case, the EC2 instance must be public so

106
00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:33.510
that the edge locations can access your EC2 instance,

107
00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:36.810
and it's possible for you to customize a security group

108
00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:41.340
so that you only allow the public ips of the edge locations.

109
00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.820
It's also possible for you to expose your application

110
00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:45.690
through an applicational bouncer.

111
00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.700
Again, the ALB must be public.

112
00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.800
The EC2 instances can be private,

113
00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.560
and therefore on the security group of the ALB,

114
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.410
you allow the public ips of the edge locations

115
00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.570
and then you now have no more security

116
00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.973
between your EC2 instances and your ALB.

117
00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:03.922
There's also a trick to even further

118
00:05:03.922 --> 00:05:08.520
and increase the security of your ALB in CloudFront is that

119
00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.590
to prevent direct access to your ALB

120
00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:11.850
or your custom origin,

121
00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:15.060
you can use what's called a custom HTP header.

122
00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.499
So that CloudFront adds a very secret header

123
00:05:17.499 --> 00:05:20.126
and sends it to the ALB for every request made

124
00:05:20.126 --> 00:05:23.490
through CloudFront, and then you configure the ALB

125
00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:26.820
or your custom origin to only forward request

126
00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.630
to your backend if that custom HTP header is seen

127
00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.160
and therefore the header name and the value

128
00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:33.690
must be kept secrets.

129
00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:34.680
So how does that look?

130
00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:38.250
Well, you have your users accessing CloudFront,

131
00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:41.490
CloudFront they will perform this kind of HTP request

132
00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:45.090
and CloudFront is going to ha to add a custom header

133
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.310
with a secret value.

134
00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.610
And then your origin, would it be your application answer

135
00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.720
or your custom origin will have some logic so that anything

136
00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.490
that does not have this custom header

137
00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.521
and this custom value will be discarded,

138
00:05:58.521 --> 00:06:01.620
else it will be forwarded to your backend

139
00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.330
and therefore any user trying

140
00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:06.630
to directly access your origin will get rejected

141
00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.480
because that custom header will not be present.

142
00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:10.380
On top of it,

143
00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.650
you can also restrict from a network perspective

144
00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.530
access to your origin by using security groups

145
00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:20.590
and only allowing the CloudFront public IP addresses.

146
00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:23.700
Okay, finally, we have origin groups.

147
00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.910
So this is to increase high availability and do failover.

148
00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.800
So an origin group has one primary

149
00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.870
and one secondary origin so

150
00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.120
that if the primary origin fails,

151
00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.190
then the second one is being used.

152
00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:36.780
And the beautiful thing is that the origins

153
00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.850
can be cross AWS region,

154
00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.931
which is good for disaster recovery.

155
00:06:40.931 --> 00:06:42.701
So we have CloudFront

156
00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:45.870
and say we have one origin group in US-east-1

157
00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:47.880
that's our primary origin,

158
00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.200
and then the second origin origin B in EU-west-1.

159
00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.960
Therefore, we have cross region high availability.

160
00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.335
So the request will be made to the primary origin,

161
00:06:57.335 --> 00:06:59.950
but in case there is an error status code,

162
00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:02.269
then the same request is going to be sent

163
00:07:02.269 --> 00:07:07.170
to our second origin and hopefully we get an okay back.

164
00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:09.750
It's also possible to do this with S3 buckets

165
00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.790
so that you have region level high availability

166
00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:12.900
for S3 buckets.

167
00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.640
So again, you would have a primary origin

168
00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:17.490
and a secondary origin for your S3 buckets,

169
00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.170
but you would set up a replication

170
00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:22.410
between the two buckets so that if one bucket is down,

171
00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:24.840
then CloudFront can try the same request

172
00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.300
on the second bucket in another region

173
00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.880
that hopefully should have data up to date

174
00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:30.900
thanks to replication.

175
00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:33.491
And that should be up thanks to availability.

176
00:07:33.491 --> 00:07:36.810
Okay, so that's it for CloudFront, the first part,

177
00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.810
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

